    "q-80841": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80841",
      "name": "Question 16",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A=\\{x\\in Z∣-1\\le x+2\\le 3\\}$ and $B=\\{x\\in Z∣{x}^{2}<2\\}?$\n\nI. $A⊆B$\n\nII. $B⊆A$\n\nIII. $B⊈A$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the integer numbers that satisfy the inequality $-1\\le x+2\\le 3,$ that is to say, $-3\\le x\\le 1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\{-3,-2,-1,0,1\\}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}<2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB=\\{-1,0,1\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false. For example, $-3\\in A$ but $-3\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true, while $B⊈A$ is false. Indeed, every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
