    "q-37262": {
      "dom_id": "step-q37262",
      "name": "Question 17",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A=\\{n\\in N∣n+1\\ge 2\\}$ and $B=\\{n\\in Z∣n-1\\ge -2\\}?$\n\nI. $A⊆B$\n\nII. $∅⊈A$\n\nIII. $B⊈A$\n\nIV. $B⊇A$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $n+1\\ge 2,$ that is to say, $n\\ge 1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\{1,2,3,4,5,6,…\\}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $n-1\\ge -2,$ That is to say, $n\\ge -1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB=\\{-1,0,1,2,3,4,5,…\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is true. Every element in the set $A$ is an element of the set $B.$\n\n- $∅⊈A$ is false. The null set $∅=\\{\\}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\n- $B⊈A$ is true. For example, $0\\in B$ but $0\\notin A.$\n\n- $B⊇A$ is equivalent to $A⊆B,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-37262-a",
              "type": "text",
              "value": "II and III only",
              "html": "II and III only"
            },
